"""Authored prose. Location/appearance text is observable, never a guilt cue.

Character life portraits are private to that character's planner. They do not
replace the causal journal or confer knowledge of another person's actions.
"""

PLACES = {
 'archive': "The lamps have been turned up too high. Above their yellow circles the archive disappears into beams and soot, and each time the wind presses against the casement the flames lean together. A desk stands under the window, its blotter crowded by ordinary papers. Beyond it are shelves, a cold fireplace and a service door whose lower edge has worn a pale arc into the boards. From the scullery comes the intermittent complaint of a kettle. The narrow opening catches a line of light from the adjoining kitchen.",
 'hall': "Meltwater drops steadily from the coats along the wall. The entrance hall smells of damp wool, lamp oil and the polish used on the visitors' book stand. One passage leads back to the archive, another to the scullery; the courtyard door knocks softly against its latch. Farther along, the ninth ward remains bolted. There is room for two people to pass comfortably here, provided neither chooses to stand in the way.",
 'scullery': "The scullery is warmer than the rest of the house, but the warmth ends at the floor. A range holds a kettle and a blackened pan; a wash basin waits beneath the window. Beside the range, the service hatch opens onto a sliver of the archive desk. Standing at it, you can see how a person might notice a sleeve or a moving hand and miss the face above it. Doors return to the archive and entrance hall.",
 'courtyard': "Snow collects along the courtyard walls without yet settling in the wheel ruts. A gate lamp turns the falling flakes into brief bright scratches. Behind you Mercy House has too many dark windows; ahead, the road drops toward the square. A narrower way runs beside the wall to Dev's winter garden. A kitchen clang carries into the courtyard and is gone almost before you turn toward it.",
 'square': "The square has emptied before the shops have quite closed. Shutters answer one another across the road, and a horse drinks noisily at the trough beneath the notice board. The Pine Lantern keeps a strip of light under its door. Paths lead toward the chapel, the Das and Rao households, and the Mercy House courtyard; beyond the last lamp the forest road becomes a different shade of black. You remember this crossing from childhood, though the signs above the shops have changed.",
 'garden': "Dev's house shelters behind winter beds edged with salvaged slate. Snow has softened most of the garden into one surface, broken by the tool shed and the kitchen table visible under a lean-to. Somewhere under the beds are last month's bulbs; from where you stand you cannot tell whether anything more recent lies beneath them. A path follows the fence toward the cemetery, while the courtyard lamp remains visible behind you.",
 'das_house': "The Das parlor is narrow enough that a visitor must turn sideways to let someone pass. Tea has dried in a saucer on the parlor table. Beside the stove stands an ash bucket; across the room, a writing desk has been closed and locked. Everything has the cared-for appearance of objects kept through more winters than their owners expected. The only street door returns to the square.",
 'rao_house': "The threshold has been lowered and the study furniture set far apart, leaving a clear passage for a wheelchair. On the workbench, acoustic drawings lie under smooth stones. A correspondence drawer sits closed beneath them. From the courtyard comes the click of a loose gate fastening; the street beyond returns to the square. The wheels have left two parallel tracks in the rug beside the workbench.",
 'chapel': "The chapel has kept the day's cold. A few candles burn near the inner door, too small to warm the stone, and a register lies on the keeper's side desk. Pews make quiet islands of darkness beneath the windows. People sometimes come here because they want witnesses, and sometimes because they do not. The square is behind the outer door; a side path crosses to the cemetery.",
 'cemetery': "The oldest stones lean in different directions, as though the ground has shifted separately beneath each family. Beyond the named graves, fir needles lie over smaller unmarked mounds. The burial office is shut against the weather. Paths lead to the chapel, the winter garden and the forest edge. A line of fir needles has gathered along the burial-office step.",
 'forest': "At the forest edge the last house-light vanishes as soon as you turn your head. Fir branches move above an otherwise sheltered path. Someone has cut marks into the milestone, but weather has made them difficult to read. The wind brings the resinous smell of freshly broken branches. The paths return to the square and cemetery or climb toward the old quarry.",
 'quarry': "The quarry opens abruptly beyond the firs. Water has frozen along the exposed cuts, and a broken cart rail points into empty air. A survey hut leans beneath the bank where the wind cannot quite reach it. The forest path is your way back. A loose stone travels down the cut face, strikes twice and falls silent.",
 'ward': "Inside the ninth ward the air has the stillness of a cupboard left closed for years. Bare bed frames stand under washed-out rectangles where notices once hung. A glass-fronted key box catches your lamp; a narrow recess interrupts the plaster near the far wall. Dust has collected against the inside of the door, interrupted now by the line of its opening.",
 'inn': "The Pine Lantern's dining room has divided itself into people waiting for news and people pretending to finish supper. A lodging book rests behind the counter. Wet boots have made dark half-moons around the stove. The road outside leads back into the square, where the coach will stop at dawn if the snow allows it.",
}

PORTRAITS = {
 'mira': ("Mira has spent much of her working life being the person summoned after a decision has already become irreversible. At Mercy House she learned the language of registers, where a crowded night could be reduced to a neat line, and later learned how long an omitted sentence could continue to trouble its witness. Her return to the town is deliberate. She has carried records back with her, not certainty that the people named in them will welcome her.", "A physician's plain dark coat hangs over the chair beside her work."),
 'savitri': ("For years Savitri could enter a crowded ward and make everyone lower their voices. She remembers each winter by what had to be rationed and each household by who came asking for help. At home she has preserved the smaller habits of authority: a straightened tablecloth, a cup placed beyond a sleeve, a conversation redirected before Leena hears its most difficult part. The old ward history and Mira's threatened publication belong to her own private recollections. She fears what their disclosure will do to the life she has maintained around her daughter.", "A woman in a carefully mended dark dress keeps her hands folded around a cup that has gone cold. Her speech is measured; occasionally she corrects a small detail before answering the larger question."),
 'vedant': ("Vedant has arranged his home so he can work without asking someone else to move a chair. The accessible passages and speaking tubes at Mercy House began as practical solutions, and he remains proud of understanding what a building permits people to hear. His research, his old institutional work and his promises to Savitri do not sit comfortably together. He responds to that discomfort by becoming exact about words, as though an exact sentence could put the rest of an evening back in order.", "A scholar sits beside a workbench in a wheelchair, one hand resting on a drawing weight. He pauses to choose precise words and asks that people speak to him directly."),
 'dev': ("Dev knows the cemetery by the work it asks of his hands. He can tell which ground will flood and which family will arrive without money for a marker. His unauthorized errands for the bereaved are a source of shame as well as stubborn justification. At home Bina has made winter beds from poor soil and salvaged slate. He wants to reach that house at the end of an ordinary evening, and wants fewer questions about how he earns the kindness of grieving people.", "A groundsman holds his cap by the brim. Soil has dried in the creases of his hands; he answers practical questions more readily than questions about his reputation."),
 'sera': ("Sera measures an evening in kettles, pans and the time left before the last guest asks for food. Wages go to her mother's care before they become her own. Once, telling an employer something unwelcome cost her a position; she remembers the weeks that followed more clearly than the dispute itself. She is capable of careful observation, but being made responsible for an interpretation frightens her. She needs room to say that she noticed one thing without agreeing that it proves another.", "The cook wipes hands that are already dry on the edge of her apron. Her eyes return to the nearest door when voices rise. She starts a sentence twice before choosing its first word."),
 'leena': ("Leena teaches children who repeat household conversations without knowing what they mean. She has grown careful about what adults can do with a fragment overheard in the street. Her years with Arun and her present attachment to Mira belong to different parts of her life, neither of which she wishes to have made into a public explanation of the other. Her mother remains a familiar point of order in a town where much else has changed.", "A schoolteacher keeps one glove on and the other in her lap. She looks directly at you, then at the floor between sentences. Familiarity does not make the conversation easy."),
 'kabir': ("Kabir knows the inspector's ordinary habits: the way he folds a map, the pause before he asks a question again, the boots he refuses to replace. Six years of service and the flooded cellar have made those details part of his working life. His younger brother Nalin belongs to another set of obligations. Kabir wants to remain the dependable man at the inspector's shoulder, even when the names in a document make that duty difficult.", "Your constable shifts his notebook into the hand he uses when expecting instructions. The gesture is familiar from years of work together."),
 'anya': ("Anya has learned that a junior constable's confidence is often mistaken for knowledge. She writes times down before giving an account and would rather be asked twice than improve a memory to satisfy a superior. She has no long family history with Mercy House. That gives her fewer loyalties here, and fewer explanations for the things everyone else seems to assume.", "A young constable holds a pencil against a small notebook. She waits for a specific instruction and distinguishes what she has seen from what she has been told."),
 'nalin': ("Nalin earns money by making deliveries other people are too busy, proud or uncomfortable to make themselves. The road offers him a kind of anonymity that the square does not. His older brother's uniform has sometimes settled difficulties before they became official, a convenience he has not always resisted. He knows what he carried and where he took it; those facts need not tell him why someone ordered the journey.", "A carrier wears a scarf wound high around his neck. He looks toward the road when asked about recent journeys and lowers his voice before mentioning a customer."),
 'kamala': ("Kamala has kept the Das household running through mornings that its visitors remember only as hospitality. She knows where the paper is put, which cup leaks and which letters are meant to stay upstairs. Years in another person's house have taught her the distinction between seeing something and being invited to speak about it. She has a life outside these rooms, and no wish to lose it in someone else's quarrel.", "A housekeeper stands clear of the doorway, leaving others room to pass. She listens closely before deciding whether a question concerns her work."),
 'hari': ("Hari's work in the Rao household is mostly a succession of small practical tasks: keeping a passage clear, carrying correspondence, fetching what the professor can describe precisely without seeing it. He knows the routines of the study rather better than the research conducted there. He dislikes being hurried into agreeing with another person's account.", "A household assistant sets down what he is carrying before he answers. He asks which day or which drawer you mean."),
 'ritu': ("Ritu has watched her uncle build an independent working life inside a house visitors too often describe in terms of what he cannot do. She is protective of that independence. Protection does not give her knowledge of every page in the study, and she knows the difference even when an outsider does not.", "A woman near the study door speaks firmly when explaining household arrangements, then becomes more cautious about events she did not attend."),
 'bina': ("Bina has made the garden productive through patient attention to weather, washing water and scraps that other households throw away. Her marriage to Dev has included the ordinary labor of defending a person whose work makes respectable people uneasy. She can recognize a disturbed bed because she remembers planting it. She cannot identify the contents of a cart she did not see unloaded.", "A gardener pushes a strand of hair under her scarf. She points with an open hand and becomes impatient when an observation is repeated back as an accusation."),
 'padma': ("Padma keeps the chapel register and the quieter confidences of those who sit beside it. Privacy is part of her work, but she has had enough years of it to distinguish privacy from an invented alibi. She remembers the small movements of an evening: who came in before the candles were changed, who stayed, who left together.", "The chapel keeper speaks without raising her voice. Before giving a time, she explains what she was doing when she noticed it."),
 'raghav': ("Raghav has spent so long defending the institution that a question about one of its records can sound to him like a question about his entire life. The language of orders and official correspondence comes easily. So does the expectation that someone younger will eventually stop asking. Arun's requests for Tara's papers have given that expectation a long and inconvenient test.", "An older man in a well-kept waistcoat looks up from an unfinished meal. His reply begins formally, as though he were dictating a letter."),
 'ishan': ("Ishan repairs things people would rather replace if they had the money. The sensory fragments he associates with ward objects have brought him attention of a less useful kind. He cannot reliably explain their source or assign a face to a voice. Some residents seek him out and later pretend they have never done so. He is frightened of the forest and of becoming the explanation for a death he did not witness.", "A repairer holds a small tool between fingers darkened by metal polish. His attention seems to drift toward a sound no one has yet identified; he admits uncertainty when pressed."),
}

DETECTIVE_LIFE = """Before the district office, before the uniform, there was Mill Lane: the rasp of your father's wheel plane through the open workshop door and Tara making you wait while she tied her shoes. You left Bhairav's Hollow with a habit of looking back at the chapel turn. Twelve winters have passed since Mercy House sent the letter saying your sister had died of fever. Requests for the original papers have filled a drawer in your room at headquarters; the paper has aged more visibly than the answers have changed.

Leena used to know where you kept that drawer. You lived together for three years, separated, and learned to write letters about the practical things that remained. Her mother once set tea in front of you after Tara's funeral. Raghav's signature is familiar from replies to your requests. You remember the weight of the cup and the silence around it.

Kabir has worked beside you for six years. You remember the floodwater in the cellar where you pulled him out, but also uneventful journeys, poor meals and the little habits by which colleagues become familiar. Dr. Mira Sen's invitation brought you back on leave: she wanted to discuss an old family record. No death had been reported when you set out. Now the coach journey is behind you, the town is under snow, and you have reached Mercy House."""


def enrich(locations, people, detective):
    for key, text in PLACES.items():
        locations[key]['description'] = text
    for key, (life, appearance) in PORTRAITS.items():
        people[key]['life_portrait'] = life
        people[key]['appearance'] = appearance
    detective['life_story'] = DETECTIVE_LIFE
